// named slot. It does not admit a node declaration, because the node
// model does not record a body. The prologue and the epilogue are on every
// body, and an owner cannot declare a slot under their names. So for
// either name, and for the empty name, HasSlot returns the zero Pred,
// which a typed build refuses. HasSlot allocates the closure of the test
// over the name, one allocation.
func HasSlot(name string) Pred {
	return core.HasSlot(name)
}

// Stamper is the annotator effect: a write handle bound to its
// match's subject. A stamper writes only to its subject's bag and
// the bags of the declarations the subject declares, its parameters,
// returns and type parameters: a fact "about" a sibling is a fact
// on the subject whose value names the sibling, so every write's
// target is statically known from the trigger, which is what keeps
// invalidation edges and audit output analyzable.
type Stamper = core.Stamper

// Stamp records v under k on the subject of the match. The claim has the
// phase call's turn and plugin, and the invocation's place in
// canonical match order, which is the rule, the subject and the gating
// instance. The invocation's point reads so far are the claim's
// derivation. In a match that a directive gates, the claim has directive
// authority and the gating instance's position. In every other match,
// it has plugin authority. A write that the fact store refuses reports
// an Error at the subject's position under [RefusedStamp], and the phase
// continues.
//
// # Allocation contract
//
// Stamp allocates what the fact store keeps for the claim, and the
// invocation's derivation where the read set grew since the last
// stamp. A second key on a subject with a claim allocates three times:
// the subject's map of keys with its first group, and the claim's
// state. A first claim on a subject allocates its bag, its boxed
// identity and an entry of the store's map of subjects, whose trie
// grows by nodes the subjects' hashes decide. The key's index grows as
// it gains members.
func Stamp[T meta.FactValue](st *Stamper, k meta.Key[T], v T) {
	core.Stamp[T](st, k, v)
}

// StampOn records v under k on a declaration the subject declares: one
// of its parameters, returns or type parameters, which no trigger
// matches on their own where a directive on the subject states
// something about them. The envelope is the subject's. An identity
// the subject does not declare is refused under [RefusedStamp] at the
// subject's position, and the phase continues. StampOn allocates as
// [Stamp] does, and a refusal allocates the finding's message.
func StampOn[T meta.FactValue](st *Stamper, owned symbol.Identity, k meta.Key[T], v T) {
	core.StampOn[T](st, owned, k, v)
}

// GraphMatch is the OnGraph subject. It reads the whole scope through
// the reader of the match. It has no subject identity, so a fact read
// of the subject returns false. Its reports take a position, through
// [GraphMatch.ErrorfAt], [GraphMatch.WarnfAt] and [GraphMatch.InfofAt],
// because a finding without a position is a defect of its reporter.
type GraphMatch = core.GraphMatch

// OnGraph runs the handler once per phase call, for logic that spans
// subjects. It takes the Emitter only. A stamper writes to the bag of
// its subject, and a graph rule has no subject. An annotator that needs
// facts on many declarations subscribes to their kinds. OnGraph
// allocates the leaf of the rule and the closure of its invocation over
// the handler, two allocations.
func OnGraph(h func(*GraphMatch, *Emitter) error) Rule {
	return core.OnGraph(h)
}

// EmitMatch is the OnEmit subject. It is one emit value that a plugin
// of an earlier turn produced in the same plan. Its subject is the
// origin of the value, so fact reads, predicates and the skip directive
// resolve against the origin. Its reports have the position of the
// origin.
type EmitMatch = core.EmitMatch

// OnEmit runs the handler once for each emit value of one kind,
// wherever a slot contains it. It takes the Emitter only. Emit values
// belong to one plan, and plans run in parallel, so a fact stamped from
// the emit side would be visible to one plan alone. An annotator stamps
// a fact about generated output on its origin. OnEmit allocates the leaf
// of the rule and the closure of its invocation over the handler, two
// allocations.
func OnEmit(k symbol.Kind, h func(*EmitMatch, *Emitter) error) Rule {
	return core.OnEmit(k, h)
}

// PackageMatch is the OnPackage subject. It is one package of the scope
// with its files and their declarations, so a handler checks the
// declarations of a package as one set. Its reports have the position of
// the package.
type PackageMatch = core.PackageMatch

// OnPackage runs the handler once for each package in scope, or once for
// each gating instance of a directive on a package under a Directive
// wrapper. The effect parameter selects the role, as it does for the
// triggers of the declaration kinds. A handler walks the declarations of
// the package without a tracked read, so the invocation records a package
// edge, and a change of any member of the package runs the handler again.
//
// # Allocation contract
//
// OnPackage allocates the leaf of the rule and the closure of its
// invocation over the handler, two allocations. The first invocation of a
// phase call allocates the tracked reader of the lane, which records the
// package edge.
func OnPackage[E Effect](h func(*PackageMatch, E) error) Rule {
	return core.OnPackage[E](h)
}
